<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anscarids</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as House of Ivrea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anscarids were a dynasty descended from Anscar I, margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivrea">Ivrea</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the late 9th century. Its Italian line produced Berengar II and Adalbert, kings of Italy in the 950s, while a Burgundian branch became counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. Through Raymond of Burgundy another branch ruled León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> as the House of Ivrea from 1126 to 1369.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anscarids.jpg" alt="Anscarids" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_Bourgogne-comt%C3%A9_ancien(aigle).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family's fortunes began with Anscar, a petty Burgundian count. With the support of Fulk the Venerable, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a>, he brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-iii-of-spoleto">Guy III of Spoleto</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> to be crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 887. When the plot failed, Anscar and his brother went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with Guy and 500 Burgundian troops to seek the throne of Berengar I, and Guy rewarded him with the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivrea">Ivrea</a>. Anscar's descendants held the march until 1030.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Anscar's grandson Berengar seized the Italian throne in 950 after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> and imprisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>'s widow Adelaide when she refused to marry his son Adalbert II. Emperor Otto I forced him to do homage in 952 and formally deposed father and son in 963. From 1002 to 1014 Arduin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> held the Italian throne in opposition to the German Henry II. Adalbert fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and his son Otto William, adopted by Otto-Henry, Duke of Burgundy, became the first count of the County of Burgundy, created in 982.</p><p>Renaud III used the title franc-comte from 1127 to mark his independence of the emperor, though he had to submit to Conrad III. His heiress Beatrice married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, and the county passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, returning to the house when Hugh of Chalon married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-countess-of-vermandois">Adelaide</a>, countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. The main line ended with Otto IV in 1303. A younger line, Chalon-Arlay, acquired the principality of Orange and ended with Philibert of Chalon in 1530.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Raymond, fourth son of Count William I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, travelled to Castile-León in the late 11th century and married the future queen Urraca. Their son Alfonso VII succeeded her, and the cadet Castilian branch ruled the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> from 1111 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León until 1369. The monarchs of Castile and León remained their male-line descendants until the 16th century, for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trastamara">House of Trastámara</a>, which took the crown in the late 14th century and ruled at times in Castile, Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, was an illegitimate cadet branch of the family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anscarids” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Anscar's descendants, the <b>Anscarids</b>, held it for more than a century.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-ivrea">March of Ivrea</a></li></ul></section>
